## TLDR Problem this solves: - `lite login` can only authenticate through proxy-mediated SSO. - The proxy never tells clients which identity provider it trusts. - CLI users on headless machines have no device-code login path. - Tokens are proxy-minted, so the IdP session is invisible to LiteLLM. How it solves it: - Proxy publishes issuer, client id and scopes on its discovery endpoint. - CLI reads that anchor and talks to the IdP directly. - Adds browser (PKCE + loopback redirect) and device-code login flows. - Stores the credential at 0600 and refreshes it transparently. - Falls back to today's SSO flow when the proxy advertises nothing. ## Relevant issues <!-- none known --> ## Linear ticket <!-- none --> ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) Notes on the unchecked boxes: - `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` are clean on every changed file, and the tests below pass. `make pre-commit` still fails on this branch against the repo-wide lint budgets (`reportMissingTypeStubs` at 83 versus a ceiling of 41, plus the ruff-strict and type-discipline budgets). Those breaches are pre-existing rather than introduced here; basedpyright reports zero `reportMissingTypeStubs` on the files this PR touches. CI disagrees with the local run and is the authority here: `lint`, `code-quality` and `budget-ratchet` all pass on this head, along with every test job. The one red check is `osv-scan`, which flags `cryptography` 48.0.1 in `uv.lock` (PYSEC-2026-3552/3553/3554). This PR does not touch `uv.lock`, the same pin is present on `litellm_internal_staging`, and every other open PR against that base is failing the same check, so it needs a dependency bump on the base branch rather than anything here. - Scope is one coherent feature but spans two layers: the proxy advertises the trust anchor, and the CLI consumes it. They are useless apart, so they ship together. The commits are ordered proxy-first and each builds independently if a reviewer prefers to split. ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Run against a live Keycloak 26.4 in Docker, at commit `088f5707fb`. Keycloak holds realm `lite` with a public client `litellm-native` (PKCE S256 enforced server-side, no client secret) and a user `alice`. The proxy runs on `:4000` against the config in `general_settings.litellm_jwtauth` **1. The proxy advertises the trust anchor** ``` $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4000/.well-known/litellm-ui-config | jq .native_oidc { "issuer": "http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite", "client_id": "litellm-native", "scopes": ["openid", "profile", "email", "offline_access"] } ``` **2. Keycloak omits `none` from its auth-method list, which is what the first commit fixes** ``` $ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite/.well-known/openid-configuration \ | jq '{token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported, code_challenge_methods_supported}' { "token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported": [ "private_key_jwt", "client_secret_basic", "client_secret_post", "tls_client_auth", "client_secret_jwt" ], "code_challenge_methods_supported": ["plain", "S256"] } ``` `litellm-native` is a public client and Keycloak accepts it with no authentication at all, but `none` is absent from that list. Before the fix the CLI read the list as "what a client must send" and hard-failed here with `provider does not support public clients`, so no Keycloak login was possible **3. Browser login (PKCE + loopback redirect) succeeds** ``` $ lite --base-url http://127.0.0.1:4000 login --flow browser Signing in via http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite (browser flow) Open this URL to sign in: http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite/protocol/openid-connect/auth?response_type=code&client_id=litellm-native&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A54339%2Foauth%2Fcallback&scope=openid+profile+email+offline_access&state=...&code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256 Waiting for the browser login to complete... Login successful! Issuer: http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite Scopes: openid profile email offline_access You can now use the CLI without specifying --api-key ``` ``` $ lite whoami Authenticated (native OIDC) Proxy: http://127.0.0.1:4000 Issuer: http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite Client ID: litellm-native Scopes: openid profile email offline_access $ stat -f "%Sp" ~/.litellm/token.json -rw------- $ jq '{auth_type, issuer, client_id, schema_version}' ~/.litellm/token.json { "auth_type": "native_oidc", "issuer": "http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite", "client_id": "litellm-native", "schema_version": 2 } ``` **4. The Keycloak-issued token is accepted by the proxy** ``` $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}
' http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $(lite auth print-token)" \ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"mock-gpt","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"say hi"}]}' HTTP 200 ``` No proxy-minted key anywhere: the bearer is the raw Keycloak access token, and the proxy validates it through `enable_jwt_auth` against the realm's JWKS **5. Refresh after IdP-side revocation, which is what the second commit fixes** ``` $ docker exec lite-oidc-kc kcadm.sh create users/$AID/logout -r lite LOGOUT OK # force the cached access token past its deadline so the CLI must refresh $ lite auth print-token; echo "rc=$?" Token expired and could not be refreshed (the identity provider revoked or expired this session). Run 'lite login' again. rc=1 ``` The underlying rejection, confirming this is a revocation and not a malformed request: ``` $ curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' -X POST \ http://127.0.0.1:8080/realms/lite/protocol/openid-connect/token \ -d grant_type=refresh_token -d client_id=litellm-native \ --data-urlencode "refresh_token=$RT" {"error":"invalid_grant","error_description":"Stale token"} HTTP 400 ``` The stored refresh token was a genuine offline token (`typ` decodes to `Offline`) and Keycloak still listed offline sessions for the client, so the grant was not deleted; the admin logout bumped the user's `notBefore`, which invalidates every token issued before it. `offline_access` therefore survives token expiry and proxy restarts but deliberately does not survive an admin-initiated revocation, and forcing a fresh login there is the correct behaviour. Before the fix the CLI surfaced only `token endpoint returned OAuth error 'invalid_grant' (HTTP 400)` Automated coverage on top of that: ``` $ uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/proxy/client/cli/ \ tests/test_litellm/proxy/discovery_endpoints/ \ tests/test_litellm/litellm_core_utils/test_native_oidc_validation.py \ tests/test_litellm/litellm_core_utils/test_cli_token_utils.py -q 768 passed, 1 warning in 211.15s (0:03:31) ``` `tests/test_litellm/proxy/client/cli/test_native_oidc_e2e.py` additionally runs a real loopback HTTP server standing in for both the proxy and the IdP: it serves the discovery document and the OpenID provider configuration, redirects through `/authorize`, and issues tokens from `/token` for the authorization-code, refresh-token and device-code grants, verifying the PKCE challenge server-side. Only `webbrowser.open` is stubbed; the redirect is followed the way a browser would follow it **Two things I did not do, stated plainly:** the model in step 4 is a `mock_response` entry, so it proves the auth path end to end but does not spend real provider money; there was no provider key available in this environment. And the click-through on Keycloak's own login form in step 3 was scripted with `curl` against the form action rather than performed by hand, so the CLI's loopback callback server, the PKCE exchange and Keycloak's server-side verification are all real, but a human did not physically type into the login page. Both are worth redoing before merge ## Type 🆕 New Feature ## Changes **Proxy: advertise the trust anchor** - `litellm/proxy/_types.py`: adds `native_oidc_issuer`, `native_oidc_client_id`, `native_oidc_scopes` to the JWT auth settings. - `litellm/proxy/discovery_endpoints/ui_discovery_endpoints.py`: publishes a `native_oidc` object on `/.well-known/litellm-ui-config`. - Fails closed. It is published only when `enable_jwt_auth` is exactly `True` and every field validates. Configuration that is present but broken is omitted and warns once, so an unauthenticated caller cannot flood the logs or read back the rejected values. **CLI: a public native OAuth client** - New `litellm/proxy/client/cli/native_oidc/` package: metadata discovery, PKCE, loopback callback listener, token parsing, device flow, browser flow, credential storage and refresh. - `litellm/litellm_core_utils/native_oidc_validation.py`: shared, stdlib-only validation for issuers, endpoint URLs and scope tokens. - `lite login --flow auto|browser|device|proxy`; `auto` picks device when `--no-browser` is passed or the provider only supports device. - `lite auth print-token` refreshes an expired native credential, which is what lets `lite up`'s `apiKeyHelper` survive expiry. - `lite up` and the agent wrappers (`lite claude`, `lite codex`, `lite opencode`) refresh an expired native credential too, rather than sending the user back through a browser login every time the IdP's short access token lapses. A key the caller supplied through `--api-key` or `LITELLM_PROXY_API_KEY` is never swapped out. The refresh window is the same window the credential's freshness is judged by, so a token that reads as stale is always still refreshable, and `lite up` picks up the key the refresh produced rather than the one it resolved before refreshing - A provider that advertises `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` without `none` is no longer rejected. The list describes what the token endpoint accepts, not what a public client must send, and Keycloak omits `none` while still accepting public clients; requiring it blocked a login that works. - A refresh rejected with `invalid_grant` now reports that the identity provider revoked or expired the session, instead of surfacing the bare OAuth error code. Keycloak answers this way after an admin-initiated user logout, which bumps the user's `notBefore` and kills even `offline_access` tokens; the only fix is a fresh login, so the message says so. The provider's `error_description` is still never echoed back. **Security properties worth reviewing** - PKCE S256 is mandatory; the CLI never holds a client secret. - Loopback redirect on an OS-assigned port, per RFC 8252. No embedded browser. - The token file is written `0600` via an atomic private write. - Refresh re-derives the whole trust chain from the stored, origin-bound `base_url`; a changed issuer or client id forces a fresh login rather than silently following the new one. - The token is verified against the proxy's `/v1/models` before it is handed out. If that check fails after a refresh, the new credential is still written but stored already expired, so a provider that rotated the refresh token cannot strand the user while an unverified access token is never used. - Refresh takes a bounded cross-process lock, so concurrent CLI invocations cannot race and burn a rotating refresh token. - Responses are capped at 256 KB with connect/read timeouts; non-loopback issuers must be HTTPS. **Out of scope, deliberately:** resource-owner password grant, DPoP, RFC 9728 protected-resource metadata, RFC 8707 resource indicators, and any browser-embedded or SAML flow. **Docs:** `litellm/proxy/client/cli/README.md` is updated. The user-facing docs site lives in `BerriAI/litellm-docs` and still needs a companion PR. ### Final Attestation - [ ] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR <!-- Left unchecked deliberately: the live-Keycloak run above covers browser login, refresh and revocation, but the completion in step 4 hits a mock model rather than a real provider, and only Keycloak has been exercised. Okta/Entra/Auth0 are untested. --> <!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --> --- > [!NOTE] > **High Risk** > Touches authentication end-to-end (discovery trust anchor, OAuth flows, token storage/refresh, and proxy JWT acceptance); mistakes could leak credentials, accept wrong issuers, or break CLI login. > > **Overview** > Adds **native OIDC** so the `lite` CLI can log in directly with the identity provider when the proxy advertises issuer, client id, and scopes on `/.well-known/litellm-ui-config` (new JWT auth settings + validated `native_oidc` payload; omitted when misconfigured). > > **`lite login`** gains `--flow auto|browser|device|proxy` and `--no-browser`: PKCE browser flow with a loopback callback, RFC 8628 device flow, or the existing proxy-mediated SSO. Auto uses native OIDC only when advertised; mid-flow native failures are not downgraded to SSO. > > IdP access tokens are stored in `~/.litellm/token.json` as `auth_type: native_oidc` (same `key` field), verified against `/v1/models` before save, and **refreshed** under a cross-process lock. **`lite auth print-token`**, **`lite up`**, and agent wrappers refresh stale native credentials without swapping explicit `--api-key` / env keys. **`is_cli_token_fresh`** now honors `expires_at` for native tokens vs proxy-minted JWT age. > > Shared **`native_oidc_validation`** (stdlib) backs proxy and CLI URL/scope checks. Docs and broad unit/e2e tests cover validation, credentials, and loopback login. > > <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit 568daa3299295df26226774d448f561aef67b604. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup> <!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->

