Available Model Group Fallbacks=None","type":null,"param":null,"code":"404"}} HTTP 404 ``` Pricing and limits the gateway now applies, from `GET /model/info` on the same proxy: ``` $ curl -s localhost:4000/model/info -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" { "max_tokens": 128000, "max_input_tokens": 400000, "max_output_tokens": 128000, "input_cost_per_token": 1.25e-05, "cache_creation_input_token_cost": 1.5625e-05, "cache_creation_input_token_cost_above_272k_tokens": 3.125e-05, "cache_read_input_token_cost": 1.25e-06, "cache_read_input_token_cost_above_272k_tokens": 2.5e-06, "input_cost_per_token_above_272k_tokens": 2.5e-05, "output_cost_per_token": 7.5e-05, "output_cost_per_token_above_272k_tokens": 0.0001125, "mode": "responses" } ``` Rates, context window, endpoints and features all come from https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5.6-cyber and https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing ## Type 🆕 New Feature ## Caveats (if any) - No live completion, the model needs Daybreak approval - Responses API only, no batch, flex or priority pricing - Reasoning effort levels undocumented, so those flags are omitted - Alias entries priced from their documented default snapshots Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/ba968551fb544f6cb8d17252e3919a80 Requested by: @mateo-berri

## TLDR Problem this solves: - No native cap on usage per arbitrary caller-supplied tag identity - Existing tag budgets are dollars-only, reset on a schedule not a window - Existing tpm/rpm limits scope to key/user/team, never to a tag value - No native cap on concurrent in-flight requests per tag identity How it solves it: - A model group declares token/request/dollar/concurrency limits under `model_info.tag_rate_limits` - Enforced on every routing attempt via `async_filter_deployments`, so fallback hops are checked too - Bucketed by `epoch_second // period_seconds`, covering both daily caps and RPM-style caps - Requests/concurrency admission is atomic check-and-increment; tokens/dollars account on success Squashed from the 18 rate-limiting commits on #36459 and rebased onto `litellm_internal_staging`. Docs PR: BerriAI/litellm-docs#851 ## Relevant issues Fixes #35116 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Verified against a live local proxy (`proxy_cli.py --config ... --detailed_debug`) at commit `4206cb1e9a386b828f50d7b5cd000e1ab1c7b5c4`, real Anthropic API calls to `claude-haiku-4-5` (no mocks), `router_settings.num_retries: 0` so a rejection surfaces directly instead of being masked by an automatic retry. Config used, a chain with a 2-requests-per-60-seconds limit and a 1-concurrent-request limit, both keyed on `end_user_id`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: haiku-tagged litellm_params: model: anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5 api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY model_info: tag_rate_limits: request_limits: limits: - name: per_minute tag_id: end_user_id limit: 2 period_seconds: 60 concurrency_limits: limits: - name: inflight tag_id: end_user_id limit: 1 period_seconds: 300 litellm_settings: callbacks: ["tag_rate_limiter"] router_settings: num_retries: 0 ``` Two truly parallel requests from the same tag; exactly one succeeds and the other gets a 429 attributing the rejection to the concurrency limit specifically: ``` $ curl -s -o /tmp/r1.json -w "%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:fix-check-2" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}' & $ curl -s -o /tmp/r2.json -w "%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:fix-check-2" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}' & wait 200 429 $ cat /tmp/r2.json {"error":{"message":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded","type":"throttling_error","provider_specific_fields":{"error":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded","type":"concurrency","tag_id":"end_user_id","tag_value":"fix-check-2","limit_name":"inflight","limit":1.0,"period_seconds":300}}} ``` Sequential 2-requests-per-60-seconds behavior: 2 requests from the same tag succeed, the 3rd gets a 429 naming the tag identity and offending limit. A different tag value succeeds while the first is exhausted, confirming per-identity isolation, and a request with no `X-Litellm-Tags` header at all also succeeds, confirming an unresolved entry fails open: ``` $ for i in 1 2 3; do curl -s -w "
%{http_code}
" http://localhost:4003/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:rpm-check" \ -d '{"model":"haiku-tagged","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with exactly one word: pineapple"}]}'; done 200 200 429 {"error":{"message":"tag_rate_limit_exceeded", ... "type":"requests","tag_value":"rpm-check","limit_name":"per_minute","limit":2.0,"period_seconds":60}} $ curl ... -H "X-Litellm-Tags: end_user_id:other-tag" ... 200 $ curl ... (no X-Litellm-Tags header) 200 ``` ## Type 🆕 New Feature ## Changes - `litellm/proxy/hooks/tag_rate_limiter.py`: new opt-in `CustomLogger`. Builds a `model_info.tag_rate_limits`-driven limits index per model group, resolves tag identity from `metadata.tags`, enforces via `async_filter_deployments` on every routing attempt. Requests/concurrency admission is an atomic, all-or-nothing check-and-increment; tokens/dollars stay read-then-account-on-success in `async_log_success_event`. Concurrency slots release on success or failure with a TTL as the safety net for a leaked reservation. - `litellm/types/router.py`: new `TagRateLimitEntry` / `TagRateLimitGroup` / `TagRateLimits` models and a `ModelInfo.tag_rate_limits` field. - `litellm/litellm_core_utils/litellm_logging.py`, `litellm/__init__.py`: register `tag_rate_limiter` as a resolvable opt-in callback string, mirroring the existing `dynamic_rate_limiter_v3` registration. - `ui/litellm-dashboard/src/lib/http/schema.d.ts`: regenerated for the new `ModelInfo.tag_rate_limits` field. - `tests/test_litellm/proxy/hooks/test_tag_rate_limiter.py`: unit tests for tag identity extraction, chain-wide vs per-deployment limit resolution, enforcement, per-entry fail-open, accounting, the atomic requests race fix, concurrency reserve/release on success and failure, and the index TTL refresh for a length-preserving deployment update. ### Final Attestation - [ ] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR <!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --> --- > [!NOTE] > **High Risk** > Touches proxy admission, Redis counters, and streaming/non-streaming disconnect cleanup. Bugs here can over-admit, leak concurrency, or 429 legitimate traffic. > > **Overview** > Adds opt-in **tag-scoped rate limits** so a model group can cap tokens, requests, dollars, and concurrency per caller tag identity (e.g. `end_user_id`), not just per key/team. > > Enable via `callbacks: ["tag_rate_limiter"]` and `model_info.tag_rate_limits`. Limits are enforced on every routing hop through `async_filter_deployments`. Requests and concurrency use atomic Redis check-and-increment; tokens and dollars are checked at admission and accounted on success. Unresolved tags fail open. > > Also adds `CustomLogger.async_release_disconnect_state_hook` and proxy cleanup so client disconnects (`CancelledError` / `GeneratorExit`) release concurrency slots instead of leaking them until TTL. > > <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit 1b03682d8d478bba999a4c856ba3ebe7ab0eaaf7. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup> <!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->

