## TLDR Problem this solves: - Team admin delete restriction was UI-only, bypassable via API - No setting existed to stop team admins adding members - SCIM-provisioned team rosters could drift from the IdP How it solves it: - Adds `disable_team_admin_add_team_user` UI setting - Enforces both flags in `/team/member_add` and `/team/member_delete` - Proxy admins and org admins over the team stay unrestricted - Dashboard hides Add Member for a restricted team admin ## User Flow Before: an enterprise that provisions teams and members through SCIM cannot stop a team admin from changing their own team's roster, so the roster drifts from the IdP 1. The proxy admin opens https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=settings and turns on "Disable team admin delete team user", there is no equivalent add setting 2. A team admin opens https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=team_info&team_id=scim-team and sees no Delete icons, but "+ Add Member" is still there and works 3. The same team admin calls `POST https://litellm-domain/team/member_add` with `{"team_id": "scim-team", "member": {"user_id": "someone", "role": "user"}}` and gets `200` with the new member on the team 4. `POST https://litellm-domain/team/member_delete` with `{"team_id": "scim-team", "user_id": "someone"}` also returns `200` and removes the member, even though the setting is on 5. The next SCIM sync fights those manual edits, and any team admin can hand their team's models and budget to a user the IdP never provisioned After: the same restriction holds for both add and delete, from the UI and from the API 1. The proxy admin opens https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=settings and turns on "Disable team admin delete team user" plus the new "Disable team admin add team user" 2. A team admin opens https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=team_info&team_id=scim-team and sees neither Delete icons nor a "+ Add Member" button 3. The same team admin calls `POST https://litellm-domain/team/member_add` with the same body and gets `403` saying `Team admins are blocked from adding members to team_id=scim-team. Contact your proxy admin.` 4. `POST https://litellm-domain/team/member_delete` returns `403` saying `Team admins are blocked from removing members from team_id=scim-team.` and the roster is unchanged 5. The proxy admin, and an org admin over that team, still get `200` on both routes, so SCIM plus proxy-level administration stay the only ways the roster changes 6. A team admin can no longer grant a team's models and budget to a user the IdP never provisioned ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Run at 675a86b5b0 against a live proxy on `localhost:4000` with the dashboard dev server on `localhost:3000`, using a team-admin virtual key, an org-admin virtual key and the master key. Flags were toggled from Settings > UI with no proxy restart between steps ### Team admin blocked while the flags are on 1. In the dashboard, proxy admin turns on "Disable team admin add team user" and "Disable team admin delete team user", reload confirms `GET /get/ui_settings` reports both `true` 2. Team admin reloads their team page and the "+ Add Member" button is gone, rows and edit icons unaffected 3. Add: ``` $ curl -s -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TEAM_ADMIN_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ http://localhost:4000/team/member_add \ -d '{"team_id":"lockdown-qa-team","member":[{"user_id":"qa-member3","role":"user"}]}' -w "
HTTP:%{http_code}
" {"detail":"Team admins are blocked from adding members to team_id=lockdown-qa-team. Contact your proxy admin."} HTTP:403 ``` 4. Delete: ``` $ curl -s -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TEAM_ADMIN_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ http://localhost:4000/team/member_delete \ -d '{"team_id":"lockdown-qa-team","user_id":"qa-member1"}' -w "
HTTP:%{http_code}
" {"detail":"Team admins are blocked from removing members from team_id=lockdown-qa-team. Contact your proxy admin."} HTTP:403 # roster unchanged: 4 ['tadmin', 'qa-member1', 'default_user_id', 'qa-member3'] ``` ### Admins unaffected while the flags are on 1. Master key on the same two routes returns `HTTP:200` on add, and `HTTP:200` on delete with the roster dropping to 3 2. An org admin over the team's organization, who is also team admin of that team, returns `HTTP:200` on both routes ### Flags off restores the old behavior 1. Add flag off: "+ Add Member" reappears for the team admin, who added `qa-member2` through the modal, roster 3 -> 4 2. Delete flag off: the same team-admin key on `POST /team/member_delete` returns `HTTP:200`, roster 4 -> 3 Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/84f905a0cd1a419cb14c06559ba65622 Requested by: @mateo-berri

## TLDR Problem this solves: - Admin UI stores DB models with empty `tags`/`guardrails`/`vector_store_ids` lists - Those empty lists made every request run prompt-management hooks - With any saved prompt registered, the first prompt manager grabbed the request - Requests without `prompt_id` then 500'd: "prompt_id is required for Prompt Management Base class" How it solves it: - Empty dynamic params no longer count as a prompt-management trigger - The fallback skips prompt managers that decline a missing `prompt_id` - The sync path returns the request unchanged on missing `prompt_id`, matching async - Arize Phoenix now declines runs without a `prompt_id`, like the other managers ## User Flow Before: once any saved prompt exists on the gateway, every request against a model the admin added through the UI fails with a 500, on all three LLM endpoints, and always-on vector store retrieval never runs 1. The proxy admin adds a Vertex Gemini deployment on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models; saving from the UI stores it with empty `tags`, `guardrails`, and `vector_store_ids` lists 2. The admin registers an OpenAI vector store on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=vector-stores and adds a second deployment whose `vector_store_ids` names it, so that model always answers from the knowledge base 3. The admin saves a reusable prompt via POST https://litellm-domain/prompts (or the UI prompts page) 4. A developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions with `{"model": "<the UI-added deployment>", "messages": [...]}` and no `prompt_id` 5. They get HTTP 500 with `litellm.APIConnectionError: prompt_id is required for Prompt Management Base class`, even though they never asked for a prompt 6. The same request against the knowledge base deployment fails with the identical 500, so no retrieval ever runs and no `search_results` come back 7. Their teammates on POST https://litellm-domain/v1/responses and POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages get the same 500 from the same deployment 8. The only request that still works is one that passes `prompt_id`, which returns HTTP 200 with the saved prompt applied After: the same requests return the model's normal completion on all three endpoints, and the knowledge base deployment answers from its vector store 1. The proxy admin adds a Vertex Gemini deployment on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=models; saving from the UI stores it with empty `tags`, `guardrails`, and `vector_store_ids` lists 2. The admin registers an OpenAI vector store on https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=vector-stores and adds a second deployment whose `vector_store_ids` names it, so that model always answers from the knowledge base 3. The admin saves a reusable prompt via POST https://litellm-domain/prompts (or the UI prompts page) 4. A developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions with `{"model": "<the UI-added deployment>", "messages": [...]}` and no `prompt_id` 5. They get HTTP 200 with the model's completion 6. The same request against the knowledge base deployment returns HTTP 200, the answer drawn from the store, and `search_results` alongside it 7. Their teammates on POST https://litellm-domain/v1/responses and POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages get HTTP 200 from the same deployment 8. A request that passes `prompt_id` still returns HTTP 200 with the saved prompt applied, unchanged 9. A deployment naming a model the provider does not serve on this route now surfaces the provider's own error, such as HTTP 400 from Vertex for a live native-audio model, instead of the gateway's 500 ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5866 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Two live proxies, one booted from the merge base (7b574b9df6) on port 34811 and one from this PR's head (5c7604d7fa) on port 39217, both with `store_model_in_db: true` against a real Postgres, both driven through the identical runbook. Deployments went in via POST /model/new exactly as the Admin UI stores them, carrying empty `tags`, `guardrails`, and `vector_store_ids`: `gemini-flash-arrays` (vertex_ai/gemini-2.5-flash plus the empty lists), `gemini-live-2.5-flash-native-audio` (the customer's exact Vertex entry), `gemini-flash-kb` (`vector_store_ids` naming a real OpenAI vector store holding one note), and `gemini-2.5-flash-aistudio` (no lists, the control). A real OpenAI vector store went in via POST /vector_store/new and a dotprompt via POST /prompts, then each proxy was restarted so it loaded all of it from the DB the way a running gateway does. `$KEY` is an end-user virtual key from POST /key/generate. Every call hit real provider APIs ### Before (7b574b9df6, the merge base) #### /v1/chat/completions, Vertex deployment with UI-injected empty lists, no prompt_id 1. `curl -s -w '
