## TLDR Problem this solves: - Keys carrying tags or guardrails cannot be edited without a license - The enterprise gate fires on a field being present, not changed - The raised 403 is swallowed, silently dropping later metadata fields How it solves it: - Both key update gates compare the submitted value against the stored one - Re-submitting an unchanged premium field is now a no-op - Creation paths are untouched and still gate on presence ## User Flow Before: an operator on a free install cannot edit any virtual key that already carries tags or guardrails, because saving the form sends those fields back untouched 1. They POST http://localhost:4000/key/generate with `{"key_alias":"team-a","metadata":{"tags":["prod"]}}` and the key is created 2. They GET http://localhost:4000/key/info?key=sk-... and see `metadata` holding `{"tags": ["prod"]}` 3. They open http://localhost:4000/ui/?page=api-keys, click the key, change only its max budget and save 4. The save sends the stored `tags` back unchanged along with the new budget 5. The response is 403 saying the feature is only available for LiteLLM Enterprise users, naming `tags` 6. The budget change is lost. The only workarounds are deleting and recreating the key, or stripping the premium field over the API first After: the same edit succeeds, and turning a premium feature on still requires a license 1. They POST the same /key/generate request and the key is created 2. They GET the same /key/info and see `metadata` holding `{"tags": ["prod"]}` 3. They open the same page, change only its max budget and save 4. The save sends the stored `tags` back unchanged along with the new budget 5. The response is 200, the new budget is on the key, and `tags` is still `["prod"]` 6. Changing `tags` to a different value, or adding it to a key that never had it, still returns the same 403 ## Relevant issues Fixes #15230 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v` - [ ] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, identical on both sides. A proxy on a free install, no `LITELLM_LICENSE` in the environment, backed by Postgres: ``` DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:litellm@localhost:5433/litellm" \ LITELLM_MASTER_KEY="sk-1234" \ litellm --config config.yaml --port 4010 ``` ### Before (6fcdea03b0) 1. Create a key that already carries a premium metadata field ``` curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/generate \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"key_alias":"pr-proof-before","metadata":{"tags":["prod"]}}' ``` 2. Confirm it is stored ``` curl -s -X GET "http://localhost:4010/key/info?key=$KEY" -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" stored metadata = {"tags": ["prod"]} ``` 3. Edit one unrelated field, echoing tags back exactly as the Admin UI does ``` curl -s -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/update \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d "{"key":"$KEY","max_budget":10,"tags":["prod"]}" HTTP 403 {"error":{"message":"{'error': 'This feature is only available for LiteLLM Enterprise users: tags. ...'}","type":"auth_error","code":"403"}} ``` ### After (5c44a65960) 1. Create a key that already carries a premium metadata field ``` curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/generate \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"key_alias":"pr-proof-after","metadata":{"tags":["prod"]}}' ``` 2. Confirm it is stored ``` curl -s -X GET "http://localhost:4010/key/info?key=$KEY" -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" stored metadata = {"tags": ["prod"]} ``` 3. The same edit as the Before run ``` curl -s -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/update \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d "{"key":"$KEY","max_budget":10,"tags":["prod"]}" HTTP 200 max_budget = 10.0 | metadata = {"tags": ["prod"]} ``` 4. Control, changing the premium value is still refused ``` curl -s -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/update \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d "{"key":"$KEY","tags":["prod","eu"]}" HTTP 403 {"error":{"message":"{'error': 'This feature is only available for LiteLLM Enterprise users: tags. ...'}"}} ``` 5. Control, setting a premium field on a key that never had it is still refused ``` curl -s -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4010/key/update \ -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d "{"key":"$KEY2","tags":["prod"]}" HTTP 403 {"error":{"message":"{'error': 'This feature is only available for LiteLLM Enterprise users: tags. ...'}"}} ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - Removing a premium field stays ungated, matching the existing falsy check - #30285 fixed the falsy half of this; this covers unchanged truthy values

## TLDR Problem this solves: - CLIs cannot sign a user in through the proxy with their SSO - The proxy's OAuth server only mints MCP session tokens, rejected on /v1/* - No machine-readable contract a non-Python CLI can discover and follow - Per-user attribution needs an admin-minted key pasted into the client How it solves it: - `GET /.well-known/litellm-cli-auth`: versioned discovery contract for native clients - `/authorize?resource=<proxy origin>` renders a consent page with a team picker - `/token` then mints the same per-user credential `lite login` mints - `POST /revoke` (RFC 7009) so logout kills the refresh token - Signing in again revokes the refresh token of the login it replaces - `lite login --pkce`, `lite logout`, `lite auth print-token` use the flow - A refused renewal prints the proxy's reason on stderr once per command, and a run-out `--pkce` key says to run `lite login --pkce` again - A refresh or a revocation that reaches the proxy while its Redis is unreachable answers `503 temporarily_unavailable` instead of a false `200` or a misleading `invalid_grant`, and `lite logout` then keeps the login record, exits 1, and asks to be run again shortly instead of dropping a refresh token the proxy never revoked - Proxy-API grants only redirect to loopback; the server never picks a team - The CLI never follows a redirect when posting to the token, revocation, or registration endpoint ## User Flow Before: a developer whose team routes OpenCode through the proxy wants to sign in from their terminal with the company SSO, and the only thing that works is a key an admin mints by hand 1. They run `lite --base-url https://litellm-domain login --pkce` and get `Error: No such option: --pkce` 2. Their own CLI fetches `GET https://litellm-domain/.well-known/litellm-cli-auth` and gets 404 `{"detail":"Not Found"}` 3. They drive the proxy's existing OAuth server by hand: `POST https://litellm-domain/register` with a loopback redirect returns a `client_id`, `GET https://litellm-domain/authorize?...&code_challenge_method=S256` sends the browser through sign-in and then to the MCP connect page at `/ui/connect`, its finish button sends the browser to `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=...`, and `POST https://litellm-domain/token` with the verifier returns `access_token: "llm_session_..."` 4. OpenCode sends `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions` with `Authorization: Bearer llm_session_...` and gets 401 `LiteLLM Virtual Key expected. Received=llm_****` 5. An admin runs `POST https://litellm-domain/key/generate` for them; they paste the `sk-...` key into `opencode.json`, OpenCode replies, and https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=logs shows the spend under whatever `user_id` the admin typed 6. Anyone who copies that key is that user as far as the logs go, and the only way to stop it is the admin deleting the key After: the same developer signs in once through their browser and OpenCode runs on a credential that is theirs, scoped to the team they picked 1. They run `lite --base-url https://litellm-domain login --pkce`; the CLI reads `GET https://litellm-domain/.well-known/litellm-cli-auth` and gets 200 with `contract_version: 1`, the authorization, token, registration, and revocation endpoints, and `code_challenge_methods_supported: ["S256"]` 2. The CLI registers itself with `POST https://litellm-domain/register` (loopback redirect on an OS-assigned port, `token_endpoint_auth_method: "none"`) and opens the system browser on `GET https://litellm-domain/authorize?response_type=code&client_id=...&redirect_uri=http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback&code_challenge=...&code_challenge_method=S256&resource=https://litellm-domain` 3. Not signed in yet, the browser lands on the proxy sign-in page (SSO or username/password) and comes back to the same authorize URL, which now shows a consent page naming the CLI's loopback address, a team picker, and Approve/Deny buttons 4. They click Approve; the browser is sent to `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback?code=...&state=...` and shows "Signed in to LiteLLM. You can close this window and return to the terminal." 5. The CLI exchanges the code with `POST https://litellm-domain/token` (`code_verifier`, no client secret) and gets `access_token`, `refresh_token`, `expires_in: 86400`, `user_id`, and `team_id`; the terminal prints `Login successful!` and the credential is stored in `~/.litellm/token.json` 6. OpenCode, started with `LITELLM_PROXY_KEY=$(lite auth print-token)`, sends `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions` and gets 200 with a reply; https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=logs shows the request under their own `user_id` and the team they picked 7. Once the credential nears its 24 hour expiry, the next `lite auth print-token` renews it with `POST https://litellm-domain/token` `grant_type=refresh_token`; replaying the old refresh token is refused with 400 `invalid_grant` 8. `lite logout` sends `POST https://litellm-domain/revoke` and deletes the local file, so the refresh token is dead 9. A hosted `https://` redirect that an MCP client may register can no longer receive one of these grants: `GET https://litellm-domain/authorize?...&redirect_uri=https://evil.example/cb&resource=https://litellm-domain` returns 400 `a proxy-API grant may only redirect to a loopback address` ## Relevant issues Fixes #37332 ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5874 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [ ] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup: a local proxy on `http://127.0.0.1:51166` with Postgres, `openai/gpt-5.4-mini` with a real key, the master key from `.env`, and `PROXY_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:51166` so discovery documents name this proxy. The end-user client is OpenCode 1.18.19 driven interactively under tmux with this `opencode.json`. The reporter's Go launcher is stood in for by curl in case 3 and by `lite login --pkce` in cases 2, 4, and 5. The "browser" in those runs is a shell script registered as `BROWSER`: it signs in with the proxy's username/password form (SSO lands on the same session cookie) and clicks Approve on the consent page, then follows the 303 to the CLI's loopback listener, which is exactly what a human does in a real browser ``` python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config repro_config.yaml --port 51166 --host 127.0.0.1 --detailed_debug --use_v2_migration_resolver ``` ```json { "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json", "provider": { "litellm": { "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible", "name": "LiteLLM proxy", "options": { "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:51166/v1", "apiKey": "{env:LITELLM_PROXY_KEY}" }, "models": { "gpt-5.4-mini": { "name": "gpt-5.4-mini", "limit": { "context": 400000, "output": 32768 } } } } }, "model": "litellm/gpt-5.4-mini" } ``` ### Before (6fcdea03b0) #### Discovery contract for native clients 1. `curl -s -w ' HTTP %{http_code}
