## TLDR Problem this solves: - Health-check rows are never pruned, so the table grows forever - Background health checks over hundreds of models write ~76k rows/day - That growing table became a customer's top DB consumer at ~1900 RPS How it solves it: - New opt-in `maximum_health_check_retention_period` general setting - The existing spend-log cleanup job prunes `checked_at` older than it - Each configured retention group gets an equal share of the run budget - Unset keeps today's behavior, nothing is deleted ## User Flow Before: an admin running background health checks over ~800 deployments watches the health page get slower every week, with no way to trim the history behind it 1. They set `background_health_checks: true`, `use_shared_health_check: true` and `health_check_interval: 900` and run the gateway for a few months 2. They open https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=model-hub and the model health view keeps taking longer to load as history piles up 3. They look for a retention knob for that history and there is none, so the only option is to hand-delete rows in the database 4. Their database monitoring shows the query behind that view as one of the heaviest consumers on the instance After: the same admin sets one retention knob and the history stays bounded, with current health still reported 1. They set `background_health_checks: true`, `use_shared_health_check: true` and `health_check_interval: 900`, and add `maximum_health_check_retention_period: 30d` under `general_settings` 2. They open https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=model-hub and every model still shows its latest health result 3. Health history older than 30 days is gone, on the cleanup job's own schedule, and only one pod does the pruning 4. Their database monitoring no longer shows that query climbing as the table stops growing without bound ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5556 ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v` - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix ### Shared setup 1. Local PostgreSQL was available, and the proxy used the same QA configuration for both commits ```bash $ pg_isready && psql "$DATABASE_URL" -X -P pager=off -c 'SELECT current_database(), current_user;' /var/run/postgresql:5432 - accepting connections current_database | current_user ------------------+-------------- litellm | postgres (1 row) $ grep -E 'master_key|maximum_health_check_retention_period|maximum_spend_logs_retention_interval|disable_prisma_schema_update' /home/ubuntu/proof_config_37681.yaml | sed 's/master_key:.*/master_key: <redacted-master-key>/' master_key: <redacted-master-key> maximum_health_check_retention_period: 30d maximum_spend_logs_retention_interval: 10s disable_prisma_schema_update: true ``` 2. The seed SQL inserted three expired rows and one fresh row per model ```bash $ cat /home/ubuntu/proof_seed_37681.sql TRUNCATE TABLE "LiteLLM_HealthCheckTable"; INSERT INTO "LiteLLM_HealthCheckTable" ( health_check_id, model_name, model_id, status, healthy_count, unhealthy_count, response_time_ms, details, checked_by, checked_at, created_at, updated_at ) VALUES ('proof-alpha-old', 'proof-model-alpha', 'proof-alpha', 'healthy', 1, 0, 12.5, '{}'::jsonb, 'proof', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '45 days', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '45 days', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '45 days'), ('proof-alpha-fresh', 'proof-model-alpha', 'proof-alpha', 'healthy', 1, 0, 10.5, '{}'::jsonb, 'proof', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '1 hour', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '1 hour', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - INTERVAL '1 hour'), ('proof-beta-old', 'proof-model-beta', 'proof-beta', '