## TLDR Problem this solves: - JWT `team_allowed_routes` only matched exact routes - Every new passthrough route needed a config change - A shared route prefix could not be granted once How it solves it: - Match trailing-wildcard patterns like `/internal-models/*` - Reuses the wildcard helper key-level `allowed_routes` already uses - Applies to `admin_allowed_routes` too ## User Flow Before: a platform team registers new internal passthrough endpoints on a schedule, and each one is a 403 for their developers until someone ships a config change 1. An admin registers a new passthrough endpoint at `/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages` 2. A developer authenticating with a team JWT sends POST https://litellm-domain/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages 3. They get back 403 and the call never reaches the model 4. Someone edits the proxy config to add that one exact route under `general_settings -> litellm_jwtauth -> team_allowed_routes`, restarts the proxy, and only then does the call work 5. The wildcard `/internal-models/*` looks like it should avoid all of this, but it is treated as a literal route name and matches nothing After: the prefix is granted once, and every endpoint registered under it works immediately 1. An admin sets `team_allowed_routes: ["/internal-models/*"]` once and restarts the proxy 2. An admin registers a new passthrough endpoint at `/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages` 3. A developer authenticating with a team JWT sends POST https://litellm-domain/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages 4. They get back 200 with the model response, with no config change for the new endpoint 5. The same team JWT calling a route outside the prefix, such as https://litellm-domain/anthropic-direct/v1/messages, still gets 403 ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-4885 ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v` - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, a live proxy with JWT auth on, one wildcard grant, and two Anthropic passthrough routes, one under the granted prefix and one outside it ```yaml general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 enable_jwt_auth: true litellm_jwtauth: team_ids_jwt_field: "team_ids" user_id_jwt_field: "sub" user_id_upsert: true team_allowed_routes: ["/internal-models/*"] pass_through_endpoints: - path: "/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages" target: "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages" headers: x-api-key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY anthropic-version: "2023-06-01" - path: "/anthropic-direct/v1/messages" target: "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages" headers: x-api-key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY anthropic-version: "2023-06-01" ``` The caller is a team JWT for `internal-team-1`, a team with `all-proxy-models`, and both cases send the same body ```bash JWT=$(cat team.jwt) BODY='{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","max_tokens":32,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Say hello in 3 words"}]}' ``` ### Before (dad4c1a0fb) #### Route under the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` #### Route outside the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/anthropic-direct/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` ### After (07416344cc) #### Route under the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output, a real Anthropic response, no exact-route config entry for it ``` 200 {"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_011CeF5n4PZqyoEeapsvvype","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hello to you!"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":14,...}} ``` #### Route outside the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/anthropic-direct/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output, still denied, so the grant stays scoped to the prefix ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - Only a trailing `*` is a wildcard, matching existing route matching - `/prefix/*` does not match the bare `/prefix` route ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/f187516a5a924ceb9c833eaa7a699cc9

## TLDR Problem this solves: - `/v1/messages` strips the vendor prefix off `provider/vendor/model` ids - Upstream gets bare `kimi-k3`, answers 400 Invalid model - Same deployment works fine on `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/responses` How it solves it: - Provider resolution now runs exactly once per request, not twice - The Messages handler passes the caller's original model down the bridge - The bridge's responses marker moves to the canonical `provider/responses/model` slot ## User Flow Before: a developer routing to a provider whose model ids carry their own vendor segment gets a 400 on `/v1/messages` only 1. The proxy admin adds a deployment named `sonar` whose upstream model is `perplexity/perplexity/sonar` 2. The developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions with `"model": "sonar"` and gets a 200 with a real answer 3. The developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages with the same `"model": "sonar"` 4. That call comes back 400 with `Invalid model 'sonar'`, naming a model id the developer never typed 5. Switching the deployment to `perplexity/perplexity/kimi-k3` gives the same 400 on `/v1/messages` while the other two routes keep working After: the same `/v1/messages` call succeeds and reaches the same upstream model the other two routes reached 1. The proxy admin adds a deployment named `sonar` whose upstream model is `perplexity/perplexity/sonar` 2. The developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions with `"model": "sonar"` and gets a 200 with a real answer 3. The developer sends POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages with the same `"model": "sonar"` 4. That call now comes back 200 with an Anthropic-shaped message, from the same upstream model the chat route hit 5. Switching the deployment to `perplexity/perplexity/kimi-k3` also returns 200 on all three routes ## Relevant issues Fixes #37716 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [ ] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Real credentials for the provider in the issue were not available, so the reproduction uses the structurally identical doubled-prefix form (`openrouter/openrouter/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning`) against a live gateway upstream. Every call below is a real billed request; nothing is mocked or stubbed Shared setup, `qa_config.yaml`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: multi-segment litellm_params: model: openrouter/openrouter/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning api_base: https://<gateway-host>/v1 api_key: os.environ/GW_KEY - model_name: plain-two-segment litellm_params: model: litellm_proxy/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 api_base: https://<gateway-host>/v1 api_key: os.environ/GW_KEY - model_name: openai-bridge litellm_params: model: openai/gpt-5.6-luna api_base: https://<gateway-host>/v1 api_key: os.environ/GW_KEY general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 ``` Started with `python litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config qa_config.yaml --port 4711` The discriminator was verified against the upstream first: `openrouter/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning` returns 200 there, bare `nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning` returns 400 no healthy deployments. So a 400 naming the bare id proves the vendor segment was dropped in transit ### Before (1d7f675e52) #### multi-segment id on /v1/messages 1. Send the request: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"multi-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. It fails, naming a model id with the vendor segment gone: ``` {"error": {"message": "litellm.BadRequestError: OpenrouterException - {"error":{"message":"litellm.BadRequestError: You passed in model=nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning. There are no healthy deployments for this model... HTTP 400 ``` #### multi-segment id on /v1/chat/completions 1. Send the same deployment through the chat route: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/chat/completions \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"multi-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. It succeeds, so the deployment itself is fine: ``` {"model": "multi-segment", "content": "pong"} HTTP 200 ``` #### single-strip control on /v1/messages 1. Send a plain two-segment id, which must still have its one prefix stripped: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"plain-two-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. It succeeds: ``` {"model": "plain-two-segment", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "pong"}]} HTTP 200 ``` #### openai responses-bridge on /v1/messages 1. Send an openai-provider id, which takes the other bridge: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"openai-bridge","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. It succeeds: ``` {"model": "openai-bridge", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "pong"}]} HTTP 200 ``` ### After (6d5d62e62e) #### multi-segment id on /v1/messages 1. Send the request: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"multi-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. It now succeeds with real upstream content, flipped from 400: ``` {"model": "multi-segment", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Here's a thinking process:

1. **Analyze User Input:** The user says "Reply with exactly: pong". This is a simple instruction to output the word "pong" exactly as specified.
2. * HTTP 200 ``` #### multi-segment id on /v1/chat/completions 1. Send the same deployment through the chat route: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/chat/completions \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"multi-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. Still succeeds, unchanged: ``` {"model": "multi-segment", "content": "pong"} HTTP 200 ``` #### single-strip control on /v1/messages 1. Send a plain two-segment id, which must still have its one prefix stripped: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"plain-two-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. Still succeeds, so the single strip was not broken: ``` {"model": "plain-two-segment", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "pong"}]} HTTP 200 ``` #### openai responses-bridge on /v1/messages 1. Send an openai-provider id, which takes the other bridge: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"openai-bridge","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. Still succeeds, so moving the responses marker did not regress that bridge: ``` {"model": "openai-bridge", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "pong"}]} HTTP 200 ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - `/v1/responses` has the same double-resolution defect, out of scope here - Measured there: `groq/groq/kimi-k3` reaches upstream as bare `kimi-k3` - Repro substitutes an equivalent doubled-prefix deployment; issue's provider credentials unavailable ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR <!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --> --- > [!NOTE] > **Medium Risk** > Changes how `/v1/messages` constructs and forwards model ids into completion/Responses routing. Wrong prefix handling could mis-route or 400 other deployments, though the change is small and covered by unit tests. > > **Overview** > Fixes `/v1/messages` dropping the vendor segment on multi-segment ids (e.g. `perplexity/perplexity/sonar` becoming `sonar`), which caused 400s while `/v1/chat/completions` worked. > > The messages bridge now forwards the **original unresolved model** so provider resolution runs once inside `litellm.completion`. OpenAI thinking reroute inserts the Responses marker as `{provider}/responses/{model}` instead of prefixing `responses/` on the full id. > > Streaming `message_start` prefers the model from `response.created` when present. Tests assert the new Responses id shape and that custom-provider calls keep the unresolved model. > > <sup>Reviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit 6d5d62e62e7d9b3898e5bbc1ee95ee6a6a58d526. Bugbot is set up for automated code reviews on this repo. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).</sup> <!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY -->

