## TLDR Problem this solves: - SCIM team roster writes were swallowed, returning 200 - IdP saw green pushes, so it never retried - Aborting on the first bad write strands the rest How it solves it: - Every roster add and remove in a push is attempted - The writes that did not land are reported together, by name - The user row is not saved when a roster write fails - A unanimous 404 stays 404, a mixed batch reports 500 ## User Flow Before: an admin pushing an Okta user whose group list still carries two stale groups gets a green push, and the user ends up claiming teams nobody put them in 1. The admin sends PUT https://litellm-domain/scim/v2/Users/okta-user with `"groups"` naming `missing-team-a`, `engineering`, and `missing-team-b`, where the first and last no longer exist 2. The response is `200` with a SCIM user body, so the IdP records the push as successful and never retries it 3. The admin opens https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=okta-user and the user claims `["missing-team-a", "engineering", "missing-team-b"]` 4. The admin opens https://litellm-domain/team/info?team_id=engineering and the user is on that roster, but nothing anywhere backs the other two, so the user's own record disagrees with every team roster and the admin gets no signal that anything went wrong After: the same push fails, names both stale groups, and still lands the group that was good 1. The admin sends the same PUT https://litellm-domain/scim/v2/Users/okta-user 2. The response is `404` whose message names both writes that failed, `missing-team-a` and `missing-team-b`, so the IdP records the push as failed and retries it 3. The admin opens https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=okta-user and the user claims `["engineering"]` only, with no membership that no roster backs 4. The admin opens https://litellm-domain/team/info?team_id=engineering and the user is on that roster, because the good write still ran after an earlier one failed, so the retry only has to land the two groups the error named ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5381 ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v` - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Both legs are one uninterrupted run against a live proxy on a pinned port 4700, backed by its own Postgres, swapping only `scim_v2.py` between them. Each leg prints the blob hash of the file it is serving next to the hash git holds for that commit, so the two halves are attributable: ``` $ git hash-object litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/scim/scim_v2.py # before leg e50e9bf05373139d1c4e4f105cd06ee3416e1ba1 $ git rev-parse cde134488c:litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/scim/scim_v2.py e50e9bf05373139d1c4e4f105cd06ee3416e1ba1 $ git hash-object litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/scim/scim_v2.py # after leg bc935d09563ad0a3524ab49f22f457859b2e4a69 $ git rev-parse c3daf63a2e:litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/scim/scim_v2.py bc935d09563ad0a3524ab49f22f457859b2e4a69 ``` The push under test puts a group that exists between two that do not, so a roster write can only fail after another one has already landed. That is what separates "the whole push was rejected" from "the push kept going and told you exactly which writes it lost". ### Before (cde134488c, merge base) 1. Created the team and the user ``` $ curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4700/team/new -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"team_id":"scim-v3-mb-team","team_alias":"scim-v3-mb-team"}' -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' 200 $ curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4700/user/new -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"user_id":"scim-v3-mb-user","user_email":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com"}' -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' 200 ``` 2. Sent the SCIM push and got a success, whose `groups` lists only the one team the roster actually received ``` $ curl -s -X PUT http://localhost:4700/scim/v2/Users/scim-v3-mb-user -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"schemas":["urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User"],"userName":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com","name":{"familyName":"U","givenName":"A"},"emails":[{"value":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com"}],"groups":[{"value":"missing-team-a"},{"value":"scim-v3-mb-team"},{"value":"missing-team-b"}]}' -w '
%{http_code}
' {"schemas":["urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User"],"id":"scim-v3-mb-user","externalId":null,"meta":{"resourceType":"User","created":"2026-08-20T22:39:08.101000+00:00","lastModified":"2026-08-20T22:39:08.227000+00:00"},"userName":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com","name":{"familyName":"U","givenName":"A","formatted":null,"middleName":null,"honorificPrefix":null,"honorificSuffix":null},"displayName":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com","active":true,"emails":[{"value":"scim-v3-mb-user@example.com","type":null,"primary":true}],"groups":[{"value":"scim-v3-mb-team","display":"scim-v3-mb-team","type":"direct"}]} 200 ``` 3. Read the user back, and it claims all three ``` $ curl -s "http://localhost:4700/user/info?user_id=scim-v3-mb-user" -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' | jq -c '.user_info.teams' ["missing-team-a","scim-v3-mb-team","missing-team-b"] ``` 4. Read the real team's roster back, which holds the user ``` $ curl -s "http://localhost:4700/team/info?team_id=scim-v3-mb-team" -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' | jq -c '[.team_info.members_with_roles[].user_id]' ["default_user_id","scim-v3-mb-user"] ``` so two of the three memberships exist only on the user record, and the IdP was told the whole push succeeded ### After (c3daf63a2e, PR tip) 1. Created the team and the user ``` $ curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4700/team/new -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"team_id":"scim-v3-head-team","team_alias":"scim-v3-head-team"}' -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' 200 $ curl -s -X POST http://localhost:4700/user/new -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"user_id":"scim-v3-head-user","user_email":"scim-v3-head-user@example.com"}' -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}
' 200 ``` 2. Sent the same SCIM push and got a failure naming both writes that did not land, so all three were attempted instead of the first failure ending the push ``` $ curl -s -X PUT http://localhost:4700/scim/v2/Users/scim-v3-head-user -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"schemas":["urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User"],"userName":"scim-v3-head-user@example.com","name":{"familyName":"U","givenName":"A"},"emails":[{"value":"scim-v3-head-user@example.com"}],"groups":[{"value":"missing-team-a"},{"value":"scim-v3-head-team"},{"value":"missing-team-b"}]}' -w '
%{http_code}
' {"error":{"message":"SCIM roster sync failed on 2 of 3 team membership writes, leaving the roster partially updated. Retry the push to reconcile it. Failed writes: add scim-v3-head-user to missing-team-b: 404: {'error': "Team doesn't exist in db. Team=missing-team-b. Create team via `/team/new` call."}; add scim-v3-head-user to missing-team-a: 404: {'error': "Team doesn't exist in db. Team=missing-team-a. Create team via `/team/new` call."}","type":"internal_server_error","param":"None","code":"404"}} 404 ``` 3. Read the user back, and it claims only what a roster backs ``` $ curl -s "http://localhost:4700/user/info?user_id=scim-v3-head-user" -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' | jq -c '.user_info.teams' ["scim-v3-head-team"] ``` 4. Read the real team's roster back, which still holds the user ``` $ curl -s "http://localhost:4700/team/info?team_id=scim-v3-head-team" -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' | jq -c '[.team_info.members_with_roles[].user_id]' ["default_user_id","scim-v3-head-user"] ``` so the good write still landed after an earlier one failed, and the two groups the error named are all the retry has to fix ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats - Duplicate add and missing remove stay idempotent no-ops - A unanimous failure status is preserved, a mixed batch reports 500 - Partial roster state is possible, named in the error, and retryable - Failed writes are named in set order, so it varies between runs ## Review notes Greptile's P1 was that failing part-way through a reconciliation leaves the roster half written. That is real, and rollback is the wrong answer to it. The compensating delete can fail on its own, and it can strip a membership that pre-dated the push, so a tidy-looking undo can destroy state the IdP never asked to change The fix is to stop aborting instead. Every add and remove in a push is attempted, and the ones that failed are reported together by name, which is what makes the leftover partial state safe to leave. SCIM reconciliation is idempotent by design: the IdP re-pushes the whole desired member set, so the writes that already landed replay as no-ops and only the named failures have to succeed Set against what this replaces, a silently lost write is unrecoverable because nobody knows it happened, while a named partial failure converges on the next push. The live run above shows both halves of that: the response names `missing-team-a` and `missing-team-b`, and `scim-v3-head-team` still received its member even though a write ahead of it failed ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/043f92f2e3a041eeb796f63ad87c68c9 Requested by: @yassin-berriai

## TLDR Problem this solves: - A leaked `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` could not be replaced safely - Rotating it made every stored provider credential unreadable - Operators feared they had to regenerate all virtual keys How it solves it: - Retired keys stay readable via `LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS` - New writes always use the active salt key - One re-encryption pass rewrites everything under the new key - Virtual keys are SHA-256 hashes, so they are untouched ## User Flow Before: a proxy admin who suspects the salt key leaked has no safe way to replace it 1. They set a new `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` and restart the proxy 2. Every request routed to a DB-stored deployment fails, because the stored provider credentials were encrypted under the old key and no longer decrypt 3. Opening https://litellm-domain/ui/?page=llm-credentials shows credentials that cannot be read back 4. Their only recovery is re-entering every provider credential by hand, and they assume all virtual keys must be regenerated too After: the same admin rotates the salt key with no downtime and no key regeneration 1. They restart the proxy with the new key in `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` and the retired one in `LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS` 2. Traffic keeps flowing: stored credentials still decrypt, because the retired key is tried when the active one does not fit 3. They send `POST https://litellm-domain/credentials/migrate-encryption?mode=salt-key` and get back `{"status":"success","mode":"salt-key","report":{...,"residual_legacy":0}}` 4. They send `GET https://litellm-domain/credentials/migrate-encryption/check?mode=salt-key` and see `residual_legacy: 0` with an empty `unreadable_locations`, which attests nothing is left under the retired key 5. They drop `LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS` and restart, with every virtual key still valid because those are hashes, never salt-key ciphertext ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-4066 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Run from a scratch dir outside every checkout, with `PYTHONPATH` selecting the tree under test, so `load_dotenv()` cannot pick up a repo `.env`. Dedicated Postgres on a namespaced port, and the proxy pinned to 4066 with the listener confirmed each boot. ```bash docker run -d --name lit4066-pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=lit4066 -e POSTGRES_USER=lit4066 \ -e POSTGRES_DB=lit4066 -p 6466:5432 postgres:16 git archive ba85ba4a99 | tar -x -C $LIVE/before # unfixed tree export DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lit4066:lit4066@127.0.0.1:6466/$DB export LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=sk-lit4066-master STORE_MODEL_IN_DB=True PYTHONPATH=$TREE python $TREE/litellm/proxy/proxy_cli.py --config config.yaml --port 4066 --use_v2_migration_resolver ``` Seed, written through the real endpoints while `LITELLM_SALT_KEY=K1-original-salt-key`: a credential, a model, and a team callback var. Then a restart on `K2-new-active-salt-key` with `encryption_algorithm: aes-256-gcm` adds a second callback var, so one row genuinely mixes formats. The SSO assertion row is inserted with ciphertext from the same `encrypt_value_helper` call `persist_sso_identity_assertion()` makes. ``` callbackA = litellm_enc::V_k1emaqw8RLRwKgOwNRe... legacy nacl, under K1 callbackB = litellm_enc::v2:gcm:hd6i49FTGJprHn... AES-256-GCM, under K2 ``` Both legs start from one `pg_dump` of that seed loaded into two databases, verified byte-identical: ```bash $ for db in lit4066_before lit4066_after; do docker exec lit4066-pg psql -U lit4066 -d $db -tAc \ "SELECT md5(string_agg(x,'|' ORDER BY x)) FROM (SELECT assertion_b64 AS x FROM "LiteLLM_SSOIdentityAssertion" UNION ALL SELECT metadata::text FROM "LiteLLM_TeamTable" UNION ALL SELECT credential_values::text FROM "LiteLLM_CredentialsTable" UNION ALL SELECT litellm_params::text FROM "LiteLLM_ProxyModelTable") t;"; done lit4066_before: 3e7069c7563d32566548f23239e38de6 lit4066_after: 3e7069c7563d32566548f23239e38de6 ``` Cases 1 and 2 run in one rotation with `LITELLM_SALT_KEY=K2`, `LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS=K1`, and `encryption_algorithm` left at its legacy default. Case 1 needs the assertion store's rotation to fail, which `_rotate_master_key` logs and continues past by design, so a trigger raises on any write to that one table: ```sql CREATE TRIGGER lit4066_block BEFORE UPDATE ON "LiteLLM_SSOIdentityAssertion" FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION lit4066_fail(); ``` ### Before (ba85ba4a99) Tree assertion, so the before leg cannot be running the fix: ```bash $ grep -ch "sso_identity_assertions\|encrypt_value_in_format_of\|unreadable_locations" \ $TREE/litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/credential_migration.py 0 ``` #### Case 1: residual attestation when the assertion store's rotation fails 1. Rotate, then ask for the attestation. ```bash $ curl -sS -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4066/credentials/migrate-encryption?mode=salt-key" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" {"status": "success", "dry_run": false, "mode": "salt-key", "report": {"residual_legacy": 0, ...}} $ curl -sS "http://127.0.0.1:4066/credentials/migrate-encryption/check?mode=salt-key" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" { "residual_legacy": 0, "unreadable_locations": "<field absent on this build>", "locations_scanned": [ "cloudzero_settings", "config_environment_variables", "credentials", "mcp_server", "mcp_user_credentials", "mcp_user_env_vars", "model_table", "sso_config", "team.callback_vars", "vantage_settings", "verification_token.callback_vars" ] } ``` `residual_legacy: 0`, and `sso_identity_assertions` is not among the locations the scan visited. 2. The store's rotation did fail, and was swallowed. ```bash $ grep "Failed to rotate SSO identity assertions" proxy.log | tail -1 WARNING: key_management_endpoints.py:4539 - Failed to rotate SSO identity assertions: Error occurred during query execution: ``` 3. The assertion ciphertext is unchanged across the rotation. ``` assertion BEFORE = 3Otg8lqICUMfsYHrKQrPPr71ck... assertion AFTER = 3Otg8lqICUMfsYHrKQrPPr71ck... ``` 4. The operator believes the attestation and drops the retired key. ```bash $ env -u LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS LITELLM_SALT_KEY=K2-new-active-salt-key python -c "decrypt_value_helper(...)" SSO assertion, K2 only -> UNREADABLE (still encrypted under the retired key) ``` The captured SSO identity is now permanently unreadable, and the SSO-backed credential flow treats it as absent. #### Case 2: mixed-format callback row through a key-only rotation 1. Same rotation, reading back the row that mixes formats. ``` callbackB BEFORE = litellm_enc::v2:gcm:hd6i49FTGJprHn... callbackB AFTER = litellm_enc::taH4RIyacyrJ1hetmU85B... callbackB is AES-GCM -> False ``` The active-key AES-256-GCM value was rewritten in the legacy format by a rotation that was only supposed to change the key. #### Case 3: key-only rotation with AES in a rotation-covered table 1. A credential stored under `encryption_algorithm: aes-256-gcm`, rotated after the setting is turned back off. ```bash $ curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST ".../credentials/migrate-encryption?mode=salt-key" HTTP 200 stored cred-aes BEFORE = v2:gcm:BeGQeajRBMp... stored cred-aes AFTER = ADz2XAu3llfbx1AJ3Q... ``` Accepted, and the AES ciphertext is gone. ### After (7a7dcfb83a) ```bash $ grep -ch "sso_identity_assertions\|encrypt_value_in_format_of\|unreadable_locations" \ $TREE/litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/credential_migration.py 7 ``` #### Case 1: residual attestation when the assertion store's rotation fails 1. Identical seed, identical injected fault, identical rotation. ```bash $ curl -sS "http://127.0.0.1:4066/credentials/migrate-encryption/check?mode=salt-key" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" { "residual_legacy": 1, "unreadable_locations": [], "locations_scanned": [ "cache_config", "cloudzero_settings", "config_environment_variables", "config_overrides", "credentials", "mcp_oauth_clients", "mcp_server", "mcp_user_credentials", "mcp_user_env_vars", "model_table", "sso_config", "sso_identity_assertions", "team.callback_vars", "vantage_settings", "verification_token.callback_vars" ] } ``` The assertion is counted, so the attestation refuses to clear while the retired key is still load bearing. Four stores that were never visited before are now in the scan: `sso_identity_assertions`, `mcp_oauth_clients`, `cache_config`, `config_overrides`. 2. Positive control, so that "it now reports non-zero" cannot be confused with a check that always reports non-zero. The storage fault ends, the rotation is re-run, and the same check on the same fixed build reports clean because it genuinely is. ```bash $ curl -sS -X POST ".../credentials/migrate-encryption?mode=salt-key" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" {"residual_legacy": 0, "sso_identity_assertions": {"scanned": 1, "migrated": 1, "already_v2": 1, "legacy": 0, ...}} $ curl -sS ".../credentials/migrate-encryption/check?mode=salt-key" -H "Authorization: Bearer $MK" {"residual_legacy": 0, "unreadable_locations": []} ``` 3. Only now is the attestation clean, so the operator drops the retired key. Everything reads under the new key alone, which is the outcome the before leg promised and did not deliver. ```bash $ env -u LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS LITELLM_SALT_KEY=K2-new-active-salt-key python -c "decrypt_value_helper(...)" SSO assertion -> {"id_token": "IDTOKEN-UNDER-K1", "issuer": "ht callbackA (was K1) -> SA-JSON-UNDER-K1 | still legacy format: True callbackB (was K2) -> LF-SECRET-UNDER-K2 | still AES-GCM: True credential api_key -> sk-provider-secret-under-k1 model api_key -> sk-model-secret-under-k1 ``` #### Case 2: mixed-format callback row through a key-only rotation 1. Same rotation, same row. ``` callbackB BEFORE = litellm_enc::v2:gcm:hd6i49FTGJprHn... callbackB AFTER = litellm_enc::v2:gcm:hd6i49FTGJprHn... callbackB is AES-GCM -> True ``` Byte for byte identical, while `callbackA` in the same row moved off the retired key and stayed in its own legacy format. #### Case 3: key-only rotation with AES in a rotation-covered table 1. Same credential, same turned-off setting. ```bash $ curl -sS -w "
HTTP %{http_code}
" -X POST ".../credentials/migrate-encryption?mode=salt-key" {"detail":{"error":"Salt-key rotation would rewrite 1 AES-256-GCM value(s) in the legacy format, because general_settings.encryption_algorithm is not 'aes-256-gcm'. Set it to that, restart the proxy, then re-run the rotation."}} HTTP 400 stored cred-aes BEFORE = v2:gcm:ZgjfY7Bh0L6... stored cred-aes AFTER = v2:gcm:ZgjfY7Bh0L6... ``` Refused with the setting named, and the ciphertext untouched. Liveness was probed at both ends of every leg (`GET /health/liveliness` returning `"I'm alive!"`), and the listener on 4066 confirmed per boot. No provider API calls are involved: this feature touches at-rest encryption only, so the meaningful secrets are the stored ciphertexts and their round trip, not a completion response. ## What the salt key protects The ticket also asked what the salt key covers in the database, so to spell it out: `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` (falling back to the master key when unset) is the encryption key for values encrypted at rest, which is the DB-stored deployment params, the credentials table, MCP server and per-user credentials and env vars, SSO identity assertions and SSO config, the `environment_variables` config row, team and key callback vars, and the Vantage and CloudZero settings rows. It is not used for virtual keys: those are stored as SHA-256 hashes of the key string, so rotating the salt key never invalidates them, which is exactly why a leaked salt key does not force reissuing customer keys ## Review notes Greptile's first P1 was right. The residual scan walked every rotation-covered table except the SSO identity assertion store, so an assertion still encrypted under a retired key could never push `residual_legacy` above zero. The rotation logs and carries on when one store fails, which is exactly how that state arises, and dropping `LITELLM_SALT_KEY_PREVIOUS` on a false-clean attestation would then leave the stored assertion unreadable, with the SSO-backed credential flow treating it as absent. The table is now scanned like the others, and I grepped every writer of salt-key ciphertext to confirm it was the only at-rest store missing The second P1 was right too, and the fix is to stop conflating two axes. The re-encryption policy now carries its own writer: the algorithm pass writes through `general_settings.encryption_algorithm`, while the salt-key pass reproduces whatever format the stored value already had. The callback-vars walker no longer round-trips the whole row through `encrypt_callback_vars(decrypt_callback_vars(...))` either; it rewrites value by value, so a row mixing a retired-key value with an active-key AES-GCM one only touches the first. The regression test uses exactly that mixed row, because a single-format fixture cannot tell the two behaviors apart The same reasoning leaves one hole worth naming. The rotation-covered tables are re-encrypted by the shared master-key rotation path, which writes through the configured algorithm and so cannot preserve a value's own. Rather than rewrite that path here, a salt-key rotation now refuses to start when it would rewrite AES-256-GCM values in the legacy format, and it names the setting to fix. Making the rotation path itself format-preserving is worth a separate change, since master-key rotation has the same behavior today Smaller behavior change in the same walker: it no longer opportunistically encrypts plaintext callback vars. It only ever did that on rows that happened to also hold legacy ciphertext, and reported them as `plaintext` regardless, so the counters now match what actually gets written Greptile's next round caught a second store on the same theme, the MCP OAuth client credentials, which the shared rotation path rewrites but the scan and the downgrade preflight both missed. Adding it to the scanned set closes both at once. Re-grepping the writers from the other direction then turned up two more, the cache-config row and the config-override row that holds Vault credentials: neither has ever had a rotation path, so a Redis password or a Vault token could sit under the retired key while the check read clean. One walker now covers all the settings-dict rows, SSO config included, instead of three near-copies of the same loop The round after that landed on the same theme from a different angle: both scanners swallow a driver failure and return zero of every counter, so a store nobody could open read as a store with nothing left in it. Zero counts from an unread store mean unknown, not clean, so the check now names those stores in `unreadable_locations` and the attestation is `residual_legacy: 0` with that list empty ## Type 🆕 New Feature ## Caveats (if any) - Rotation requires a restart with both keys set - Values that decrypt under no configured key are preserved, not overwritten - Format is preserved: salt rotation never switches algorithm - Rotation refuses to start if it would downgrade AES values - A store the scan cannot read blocks the attestation instead of passing ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/b3f63359e22d4933ad032ad53c42e984 Requested by: @yassin-berriai

## TLDR Problem this solves: - Missing container file content returned 200 with an empty-looking payload - The provider's 404 body came back as opaque bytes - Only binary container endpoints were affected How it solves it: - Check the response status before the binary short-circuit - Raise the provider's error for every container file endpoint - Fall back to the response text when the error body is not JSON ## User Flow Before: a developer downloading a container file gets a 200 back for an id that does not exist, so their code writes the gateway's error text to disk as if it were the file 1. They upload a file and get back an id like `cfile_6a8786d8c4048191bfdc2c8737559715` 2. They later request GET https://litellm-domain/v1/containers/cntr_abc/files/cfile_nonexistent/content for an id that has since been removed 3. The response is `200 OK` with `content-type: application/octet-stream`, so their client treats it as file bytes 4. The bytes are actually `{"error": {"message": "File not found.", ...}}`, which they save as a 96-byte file and only discover when something downstream chokes on it 5. Asking for the same missing id's metadata at GET https://litellm-domain/v1/containers/cntr_abc/files/cfile_nonexistent does return `404`, so the two routes disagree about whether the file exists After: the same download returns a 404 the client can act on, matching what the metadata route already said 1. They upload a file and get back an id like `cfile_6a8786d8c4048191bfdc2c8737559715` 2. They request GET https://litellm-domain/v1/containers/cntr_abc/files/cfile_nonexistent/content for the id that has since been removed 3. The response is `404` with `content-type: application/json` and the body `{"error":{"message":"litellm.NotFoundError: NotFoundError: OpenAIException - File not found.",...}}` 4. Their client raises instead of writing a bogus file, and retry or fallback logic runs 5. GET https://litellm-domain/v1/containers/cntr_abc/files/cfile_nonexistent still returns `404`, so both routes now agree 6. Downloading a file that does exist is unchanged: `200` with `content-type: application/octet-stream` and the real bytes ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, a proxy on localhost:4000 with one OpenAI model and `master_key: sk-1234`, plus a container and a real uploaded file so the success path is covered on both sides ```bash curl -sS http://localhost:4000/v1/containers -H "Authorization: Bearer sk-1234" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \ -d '{"name":"pr-proof","expires_after":{"anchor":"last_active_at","minutes":10}}' printf 'hello from litellm container files
