 with Redis `cache_params`, or `general_settings.coordination_redis`) and fail closed while Redis is down: a refresh or a `POST /revoke` that arrives then answers `503 temporarily_unavailable`, the CLI keeps the key it has and tries again on its next command, and `lite logout` keeps the login record, exits 1, and asks to be run again shortly (a re-login that cannot revoke the record it replaces still stands and warns, and that previous refresh token expires on its own within 14 days); without Redis the record is per worker, so a multi-worker proxy needs Redis for a revoked or used refresh token to stay refused on every worker (documented in both READMEs and the docs page) - A `--pkce` session has no absolute lifetime: every renewal mints a refresh token good for another 14 days, so a user who runs any `lite` command at least every 14 days never signs in again until a LiteLLM-side stop (deactivated user, removed team membership), where classic `lite login` asks for a browser sign-in every `LITELLM_CLI_JWT_EXPIRATION_HOURS` - `lite logout` and `POST /revoke` burn the refresh token only; the stateless key already minted keeps working until its own expiry (`LITELLM_CLI_JWT_EXPIRATION_HOURS`, default 24h), so a copied `token.json` or a Claude Code process that already read the key is not cut off - An `/authorize` request whose `resource` is the bare proxy origin is now a proxy-API grant, so a non-loopback `redirect_uri` with that `resource` gets `400 invalid_request` where the merge base answered `303` to the MCP connect page; MCP clients send the MCP resource URL or no `resource` and keep the old path - `lite login --pkce` is loopback-only: the consent page sends the browser to `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` on the machine running the browser, so over SSH or inside a container the login ends with `Authentication failed: timed out waiting for the browser sign-in to finish` unless that port is forwarded with `ssh -L`; device flow was scoped out of #37332 and classic `