ts a bare 500 instead.** 1. The client sends `POST https://<proxy-host>/v1/chat/completions` with `Authorization: Bearer <virtual-key>` and body: ```json { "model": "<model>", "stream": true, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "..."}], "tools": [{"type": "code_interpreter", "container": {"type": "auto"}}] } ``` 2. Because `stream: true` was set, the client expects a `Content-Type: text/event-stream` response and reads it incrementally as `data: {...}` chunks ending in `data: [DONE]` — the same shape it gets for any other streaming request. 3. Instead, the client receives `HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error` with body: ```json {"error":{"message":"'async for' requires an object with __aiter__ method, got ModelResponseStream","type":"None","param":"None","code":"500"}} ``` Nothing in this response tells the client it's related to the `code_interpreter` tool or to streaming specifically. 4. The end user's chat UI shows no assistant reply for this turn — just a generic "something went wrong, please retry" state. 5. If the client (or its retry logic) re-sends the same conversation a little later — same `model` / `messages` / `tools`, this time without `stream` — it can instead get back a *different*, equally opaque `HTTP/1.1 500` whose body is an internal `"Invalid response object"` exception trace. Nothing links this second failure back to the first request, so the client has no way to know the two are related. **After a (hypothetical) fix — the same request succeeds end to end.** 1. The client sends `POST https://<proxy-host>/v1/chat/completions` with the exact same headers and body as above. 2. The client receives `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, and reads a normal sequence of `data: {"id":"...","object":"chat.completion.chunk","choices":[{"delta":{...}}]}` chunks, ending in `data: [DONE]` — indistinguishable from a streaming request that doesn't include the `code_interpreter` tool. 3. The chat UI renders the assistant's reply incrementally 